  "slug": "linked-dysfunction-7444",
  "summary": "Eight preprints link genetic, synuclein and receptor-loss models to altered striatal dopamine release, D1 pathway plasticity and behavioral deficits.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is altered dopaminergic and cholinergic control of striatal output in neuropsychiatric and neurodegenerative models. Conditional Tshz3 deletion in cholinergic neurons reduces M1 muscarinic transmission onto projection neurons and abolishes D1 versus D2 excitability differences. The 3q29 deletion model shows elevated evoked dorsal striatal dopamine release via PKC-mediated reduction. Functional analysis of 55 SLC6A3 missense variants from psychiatric patients resolves seven phenotypic classes. In 6-OHDA mice, prolonged D1 signaling loss makes direct-pathway neurons hyperresponsive to dopamine. α-Synuclein overexpression in midbrain dopamine neurons produces progressive action-outcome learning deficits, and a human Anxa1-associated dopaminergic population is most depleted in Parkinson's brains. Aralar/AGC1 loss links dopaminergic and redox dysfunction to impaired sociability, rescued by β-hydroxybutyrate. The 5-HT2A deletion study addresses a separate serotonergic and hippocampal axis.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
